### What is the Ternary Operator?

Most Java operators are either **unary** (takes 1 operand, like `++x` or `!flag`) or **binary** (takes 2 operands, like `a + b` or `a == b`).

The **Ternary Operator (`? :`)** is the only operator in Java that takes **three operands**. It acts as a compact, inline shortcut for a simple `if-else` statement.

#### Syntax:
```java
variable = (condition) ? expressionIfTrue : expressionIfFalse;
```

---

### 1. Replacing Verbose `if-else` Blocks

Compare these two equivalent pieces of code:

#### The Verbose `if-else` Way (5 lines):
```java
int marks = 78;
String status;

if (marks >= 40) {
    status = "Pass";
} else {
    status = "Fail";
}
```

#### The Clean Ternary Way (1 line):
```java
int marks = 78;
String status = (marks >= 40) ? "Pass" : "Fail";
```
It reads naturally: *Is marks >= 40? If yes, "Pass"; otherwise, "Fail".*

---

### 2. Common Practical Uses

#### Example 1: Finding Maximum of Two Numbers
```java
int a = 25;
int b = 40;
int max = (a > b) ? a : b; // 40
```

#### Example 2: Dynamic Delivery Fee
```java
int cartTotal = 600;
int deliveryFee = (cartTotal >= 500) ? 0 : 50; // Free delivery over 500
```

#### Example 3: Even or Odd Label
```java
int num = 17;
String type = (num % 2 == 0) ? "Even" : "Odd"; // "Odd"
```

---

### 3. Rules & Best Practices

> [!NOTE]
> **Rule 1: Type Compatibility**:
> Both branches of the ternary operator must evaluate to compatible data types.
> ```java
> // COMPILE ERROR: incompatible types: String cannot be converted to int
> // int result = (marks >= 40) ? 100 : "Fail";
> ```

> [!WARNING]
> **Rule 2: Avoid Nested Ternary Operators!**:
> You *can* technically nest ternary operators:
> ```java
> String grade = (score > 90) ? "A" : (score > 75) ? "B" : (score > 50) ? "C" : "F";
> ```
> In industry codebases, **nested ternary expressions are considered an anti-pattern**. They are difficult to read and error-prone to debug. When you have more than 2 outcomes, use standard `if-else-if` blocks!

---

### Code Walkthrough

```java
public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int userAge = 20;
        String votingEligibility = (userAge >= 18) ? "Eligible to Vote" : "Too Young to Vote";
        System.out.println("Status: " + votingEligibility);

        int itemPrice = 120;
        int discount = (itemPrice > 100) ? 20 : 0;
        int finalPrice = itemPrice - discount;
        System.out.println("Final Price: Rs " + finalPrice);
    }
}
```

